"GAO-10-499 2009 Lobbying Disclosure: Observations on Lobbyists' Compliance with Disclosure Requirements (https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-10-499.pdf)\nciteturn194view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 41; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-10-499.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"11,500\"}); Total lines: 1658\nL276@P6:  represent at least 20 percent of the time that he or she spends\nL277@P6: on behalf of the client during the quarter.4\nL278@P6:  Lobbying firms are persons or\nL279@P6: \nL280@P6: 3\nL287@P7: entities that have one or more employees who are lobbyists on behalf of a\nL288@P7: client other than that person or entity.5\nL289@P7: Lobbying firms are required to file a registration with the Secretary of the\nL290@P7: Senate and the Clerk of the House for each client if the lobbying firm\nL291@P7: receives over $3,000 in income from that client for lobbying activities.6\nL292@P7: Lobbyists are also required to submit a quarterly report, an LD-2 report,\nL293@P7: for each registration filed. The registration and subsequent LD-2 reports\nL294@P7: must disclose\nL295@P7: • the name of the organization, lobbying firm, or self-employed individual\nL296@P7: that is lobbying on that client’s behalf;\nL297@P7: • a list of individuals who acted as lobbyists on behalf of the client\nL298@P7: during the reporting period;\nL299@P7: • whether any lobbyists served as covered executive branch or\nL300@P7: legislative branch officials in the previous 20 years;7\nL301@P7: • the name of and further information about the client, including a\nL302@P7: general description of its business or activities;\nL303@P7: • information on the general issue area and specific lobbying issues;\nL304@P7: • any foreign entities that have an interest in the client;\nL305@P7: • the client’s status as a state or local government;\nL306@P7: • information on which federal agencies and house(s) of Congress the\nL307@P7: lobbyist contacted on behalf of the client during the reporting period;\nL308@P7: \nL309@P7: 5\nL310@P7: 2 U.S.C. § 1602(9).\nL311@P7: 6\nL312@P7: Organizations employing in-house lobbyists file only one registration. An organization is\nL313@P7: exempt from filing if total expenses in connection with lobbying activities are not expected\nL314@P7: to exceed $11,500. Under the LDA, lobbying firms are exempt from filing if they are\nL315@P7: expected to make under $2,500 from the client, and organizations are exempt if their\nL316@P7: expenses are under $10,000 during the quarterly period. 2 U.S.C. § 1603(a)(3). These\nL317@P7: amounts are adjusted for inflation, and the House/Senate Lobbying Disclosure Act\nL318@P7: Guidance lists amounts for firms and organizations adjusted for inflation as $3,000 and\nL319@P7: $11,500, respectively.\nL320@P7: 7\nL321@P7: The LDA defines a covered executive branch official as the President, Vice President, an\nL322@P7: officer or employee, or any other individual functioning in the capacity of such an officer or\nL323@P7: employee, of the Executive Office of the President, an officer or employee serving in levels\nL324@P7: I-V of the Executive Schedule, members of the uniformed services whose pay grade is at or\nL325@P7: above O-7, and any officer or employee serving in a position of a confidential, policy\u0002determining, policy-making, or policy-advocating character who is excepted from\nL326@P7: competitive service as determined by the Office of Personnel Management (commonly\nL327@P7: called Schedule C employees). The LDA defines a covered legislative branch official as a\nL328@P7: Member of Congress, an elected officer of either house of Congress, or any employee of a\nL329@P7: Member, a committee of either House of Congress, the leadership staff of either House of\nL330@P7: Congress, a joint committee of Congress, or a working group or caucus organized to\nL331@P7: provide legislative services or other assistance to Members. 2 U.S.C. § 1602(3), (4).\nL332@P7-8: Page 4 GAO-10-499 2009 Lobbying Disclosure\nL333@P8: • the amount of income related to lobbying activities received from the\nL334@P8: client (or expenses for organizations with in-house lobbyists) during\nL335@P8: the quarter rounded to the nearest $10,000; and\nL336@P8: • a list of constituent organizations that contribute more than $5,000 for\nL337@P8: lobbying in a quarter and actively participate in planning, supervising,\nL338@P8: or controlling lobbying activities, if the client is a coalition or\nL339@P8: association.\nL340@P8: The LDA also requires lobbyists to report certain contributions\nL341@P8: semiannually in the contributions report, or the LD-203 report. These\nL342@P8: reports must be filed 30 days after the end of a semiannual period by each\nL343@P8: organization registered to lobby and by each individual listed as a lobbyist\nL344@P8: on an organization’s lobbying reports. The lobbyists or organizations must\nL345@P8: list the name of each federal candidate or officeholder, leadership political\nL346@P8: action committee, or political party committee to which they made\nL347@P8: contributions equal to or exceeding $200 in the aggregate during the\nL348@P8: semiannual period. The lobbyists or organizations must also report\nL349@P8: contributions made to presidential library foundations and presidential\nL350@P8: inaugural committees. In addition, the lobbyists or organizations must\nL351@P8: report funds contributed to pay the cost of an event to honor or recognize\nL352@P8: a covered official, funds paid to an entity named for or controlled by a\nL353@P8: covered official, and contributions to a person or entity in recognition of\nL354@P8: an official or to pay the costs of a meeting or other event held by or in the\nL355@P8: name of a covered official. Finally, the LD-203 report requires lobbyists or\nL356@P8: organizations to certify that they have read and are familiar with the gift\nL357@P8: and travel rules of the Senate and House and that they have not provided,\nL358@P8: requested, or directed a gift or travel to a member, officer, or employee of\nL359@P8: Congress that would violate those rules. Each individual lobbyist and\nL360@P8: organization must file a LD-203 report each period and certify compliance\nL361@P8: with the gift and travel rules, even if there are no contributions to report.\nL362@P8: The Office is responsible for the enforcement of the LDA. The Office\nL363@P8: fulfills its administrative responsibilities by researching and responding to\nL364@P8: referrals of noncomplying lobbyists submitted by the Secretary of the\nL365@P8: Senate and Clerk of the House. The Office sends additional noncompliance\nL366@P8: notices to the lobbyists, requesting that the lobbyists file reports or correct\nL367@P8: reported information. The Office also has the authority to pursue a civil or\nL368@P8: criminal case for noncompliance.\nL369@P8-9: Page 5 GAO-10-499 2009 Lobbying Disclosure\nL370@P9: An electronic system has been developed and implemented in response to\nL371@P9: a recommendation in our prior report,8\nL372@P9:  specifically to address issues we\nL373@P9: raised regarding the tracking, analysis, and reporting of enforcement\nL374@P9: activities for lobbyists who were referred to the Office for failure to\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGAO-12-492, 2011 LOBBYING DISCLOSURE: Observations on Lobbyists' Compliance with Disclosure Requirements (https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-12-492.pdf)\nciteturn194view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 43; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-12-492.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"11,500\"}); Total lines: 1544\nL303@P7: in the LDA, elaborates on the registration and reporting requirements,\nL304@P7: includes specific examples of different scenarios, and provides\nL305@P7: explanations of why certain scenarios prompt or do not prompt disclosure\nL306@P7: under the LDA. In meetings with the Secretary of the Senate and Clerk of\nL307@P7: the House, they stated that they consider information we report on\nL308@P7: lobbying disclosure compliance when they periodically update the\nL309@P7: guidance.\nL310@P7: The LDA defines a lobbyist as an individual who is employed or retained\nL311@P7: by a client for compensation, who has made more than one lobbying\nL312@P7: contact (written or oral communication to a covered executive or\nL313@P7: legislative branch official made on behalf of a client), and whose lobbying\nL314@P7: activities6 represent at least 20 percent of the time that he or she spends\nL315@P7: on behalf of the client during the quarter.7\nL316@P7: \nL317@P7: 6 Lobbying activities include not only direct lobbying contacts but also efforts in support of\nL318@P7: such contacts, such as preparation and planning activities, research, and other\nL319@P7: background work that is intended for use in contacts.\nL320@P7:  Lobbying firms are persons or\nL321@P7: 7 2 U.S.C. § 1602(10)\nL322@P7-8: Background\nL323@P8: Page 5 GAO-12-492 2011 Lobbying Disclosure\nL324@P8: entities that have one or more employees who lobby on behalf of a client\nL325@P8: other than that person or entity.8\nL326@P8: Lobbying firms are required to file a registration with the Secretary of the\nL327@P8: Senate and the Clerk of the House for each client if the firms receive or\nL328@P8: expect to receive over $3,000 in income or $11,500 in incurred expenses\nL329@P8: from that client for lobbying activities.9\nL330@P8: • the name of the organization, lobbying firm, or self-employed\nL331@P8: individual that is lobbying on that client’s behalf;\nL332@P8: Lobbyists are also required to\nL333@P8: submit a quarterly report, also known as an LD-2 report, for each\nL334@P8: registration filed. The registration and subsequent LD-2 reports contain\nL335@P8: the following elements, if applicable:\nL336@P8: • a list of individuals who acted as lobbyists on behalf of the client\nL337@P8: during the reporting period;\nL338@P8: • whether any lobbyists served as covered executive branch or\nL339@P8: legislative branch officials “covered officials” in the previous 20\nL340@P8: years;10\nL341@P8: • the name of and further information about the client, including a\nL342@P8: general description of its business or activities;\nL343@P8: • information on the specific lobbying issue areas and corresponding\nL344@P8: general issue codes used to describe lobbying activities;\nL345@P8: • any foreign entities that have an interest in the client;\nL346@P8: • whether the client is a state or local government;\nL347@P8: \nL348@P8: 8 2 U.S.C. § 1602(9).\nL349@P8: 9 Organizations employing in-house lobbyists file only one registration. An organization is\nL350@P8: exempt from filing if total expenses in connection with lobbying activities are not expected\nL351@P8: to exceed $11,500. Amounts are adjusted for inflation and published in the LDA guidance.\nL352@P8: 10 The LDA defines a covered executive branch official as the President, Vice President,\nL353@P8: an officer or employee, or any other individual functioning in the capacity of such an officer\nL354@P8: or employee of the Executive Office of the President; an officer or employee serving in\nL355@P8: levels I through V of the Executive Schedule; members of the uniformed services whose\nL356@P8: pay grade is at or above O-7; and any officer or employee serving in a position of a\nL357@P8: confidential, policy-determining, policymaking, or policy-advocating character who is\nL358@P8: excepted from competitive service as determined by the Office of Personnel Management\nL359@P8: (commonly called Schedule C employees). The LDA defines a covered legislative branch\nL360@P8: official as a member of Congress, an elected officer of either house of Congress, or any\nL361@P8: employee or any other individual functioning in the capacity of an employee of a member,\nL362@P8: a committee of either House of Congress, the leadership staff of either House of\nL363@P8: Congress, a joint committee of Congress, or a working group or caucus organized to\nL364@P8-9: provide legislative services or other assistance to members. 2 U.S.C. § 1602(3), (4).\nL365@P9: Page 6 GAO-12-492 2011 Lobbying Disclosure\nL366@P9: • information on which federal agencies and House(s) of Congress the\nL367@P9: lobbyist contacted on behalf of the client during the reporting period;\nL368@P9: • the amount of income related to lobbying activities received from the\nL369@P9: client (or expenses for organizations with in-house lobbyists) during\nL370@P9: the quarter rounded to the nearest $10,000; and\nL371@P9: • a list of constituent organizations that contribute more than $5,000 for\nL372@P9: lobbying in a quarter and actively participate in planning, supervising,\nL373@P9: or controlling lobbying activities, if the client is a coalition or\nL374@P9: association.\nL375@P9: The LDA, as amended, also requires lobbyists to report certain\nL376@P9: contributions semiannually in the LD-203 report. These reports must be\nL377@P9: filed 30 days after the end of a semiannual period by each lobbying firm\nL378@P9: registered to lobby and by each individual listed as a lobbyist on a firm’s\nL379@P9: lobbying reports. The lobbyists or lobbying firms must\nL380@P9: • list the name of each federal candidate or officeholder, leadership\nL381@P9: political action committee, or political party committee to which they\nL382@P9: made contributions equal to or exceeding $200 in the aggregate\nL383@P9: during the semiannual period;\nL384@P9: • report contributions made to presidential library foundations and\nL385@P9: presidential inaugural committees;\nL386@P9: • report funds contributed to pay the cost of an event to honor or\nL387@P9: recognize a covered official, funds paid to an entity named for or\nL388@P9: controlled by a covered official, and contributions to a person or entity\nL389@P9: in recognition of an official or to pay the costs of a meeting or other\nL390@P9: event held by or in the name of a covered official; and\nL391@P9: • certify that they have read and are familiar with the gift and travel\nL392@P9: rules of the Senate and House and that they have not provided,\nL393@P9: requested, or directed a gift or travel to a member, officer, or\nL394@P9: employee of Congress that would violate those rules.\nL395@P9: The Secretary of the Senate and the Clerk of the House, along with the\nL396@P9: Office are responsible for ensuring compliance with the LDA. The\nL397@P9: Secretary of the Senate and the Clerk of the House notify lobbyists or\nL398@P9: lobbying firms in writing that they are not complying with reporting\nL399@P9: requirements in the LDA, and subsequently refer those lobbyists who fail\nL400@P9: to provide an appropriate response to the Office. The Office researches\nL401@P9: these referrals and sends additional noncompliance notices to the\nL402@P9: lobbyists, requesting that the lobbyists file reports or correct reported\nL403@P9: information. If the Office does not receive a response after 60 days, it\nL404@P9: decides whether to pursue a civil or criminal case against each\n"
